 <section id="overview"><h2>What the result says</h2><p>A billiard trajectory inside an ellipse bounces according to the usual reflection law. In a confocal family, every side touches the same inner ellipse, called the caustic. Moving the starting point changes the polygon’s phase while retaining that caustic.</p><p>This note asks when the product of the distances from all polygon vertices to one fixed focus stays unchanged as the phase moves. The answer depends on the least period: the number of bounces before the trajectory first returns. Counting a repeated traversal as a longer list can give the wrong answer.</p></section>
 <section id="theorem"><h2>The full theorem</h2><p>Fix outer and inner ellipses x²/a²+y²/b²=1 and x²/A²+y²/B²=1, with a&gt;b&gt;0, A&gt;B&gt;0, a&gt;A, b&gt;B and a²−b²=A²−B²=c²&gt;0. The caustic is strictly nested, nondegenerate and confocal. The foci F±=(±c,0) are fixed, and the factors are ordinary positive Euclidean distances from the original billiard vertices, not squared distances or distances from an outer tangent polygon.</p><p>For any actual periodic elastic billiard family tangent to that caustic, let n be its primitive least period and set Dₙ±(u)=∏ⱼ₌₀ⁿ⁻¹ |Pⱼ(u)−F±|. Each focal product is independent of phase if and only if n≡2 (mod 4). Every admissible primitive coprime star winding and both orientations are included; n=1 and n=2 cannot occur in this strict elliptical-caustic setting.</p><p>For every even least period, Dₙ⁺(u)=Dₙ⁻(u) at each phase. This includes n≡0 (mod 4), when the equal products still vary with phase. Equality between the two foci is a different assertion from phase constancy.</p><p>For every integer r≥1, an r-fold traversal gives (Dₙ±)ʳ. Positivity makes this power operation injective, so repetition preserves the primitive constancy class in both directions. For a listed return length N≡2 (mod 4), constancy holds exactly when its least period is even.</p></section>
 <section id="proof"><h2>Why least period controls the product</h2><p>The proof uses Stachel’s classical Jacobi parametrization P(u)=(−a sn u,b cn u), with modulus k=c/A∈(0,1), K=K(k), K′=K(√(1−k²)), cn(v)=B/b and 0&lt;v&lt;K. Here a=A dn(v)/cn(v), and successive vertices are separated by δ=2v=4Kτ/n, where gcd(τ,n)=1 and 0&lt;τ&lt;n/2. Reversing orientation gives the same cyclic subgroup.</p><p>The manuscript checks that the tangent contacts lie inside the actual chords and that the two inward rays satisfy elastic reflection across the normal. Real injectivity modulo 4K makes n the true vertex period. The geometric calculation gives the positive ordinary-distance factors g±(u)=a±c sn u; a&gt;c prevents a hidden sign or square-root convention.</p><p>On the sn torus ℂ/(4Kℤ+2iK′ℤ), the factor g₊ has two simple poles p=iK′ and p+2K, and two distinct simple zeros p−K±v. These are complete, because zero and pole degrees agree. This torus is used for the focal factor; the full complex boundary map need not have the same imaginary period.</p><p>Writing H=⟨δ⟩ for the n-element orbit subgroup, translating the complete divisor through H gives one double zero coset, represented by z=−1/4+τ/(2n), and pole cosets H and 1/2+H. For odd n there are 2n uncancelled simple poles. For even n there are n double poles, cancelled exactly when (−n+2τ)/4 is an integer: precisely n≡2 (mod 4).</p><p>A divisor-free meromorphic function on the compact connected torus is constant. In the other classes a pole remains; the identity theorem transfers that nonconstancy to the physical real family. The half-period identity g₋(u)=g₊(u+2K) gives the second focus and the even-period pointwise equality. This is an all-period proof, rather than an extrapolation from finite checks.</p><p>For n=2m≡2 (mod 4), the positive constant can be evaluated as ∏ⱼ₌₀ᵐ⁻¹ [a²−c² sn²(2jv)]. It has units of lengthⁿ.</p></section>
 <section id="triangle"><h2>Why a listed return length of six is insufficient</h2><p>Take a=√15, b=√7, c=√8, A²=135/16 and B²=7/16. The triangle with vertices (a,0), (−3a/4,7/4), (−3a/4,−7/4), and its central reflection, are primitive three-bounce trajectories in the same fixed confocal family. Their side lines x=−A and x±ay=a are tangent to the caustic, their contact points lie inside the segments, and the inward unit rays satisfy the physical reflection law.</p><p>At the fixed focus F₊ their positive primitive products are 15a/2+3c and 15a/2−3c. Traversing each triangle twice produces six listed events, but the two products differ by (15a/2+3c)²−(15a/2−3c)²=90ac=180√30&gt;0.</p><p>Thus a return length of six alone does not force phase constancy. The example distinguishes an unrestricted repeated-list interpretation from a statement explicitly about primitive least period; it does not refute that primitive statement.</p></section>
 <section id="limits"><h2>Circle and other boundaries</h2><p>For a circle the two foci coincide with its center, every focal distance is the radius a, and every listed N-event product is aᴺ. This is a separate elementary case: the noncircular pole argument is not continued through c=0.</p><p>Hyperbolic or degenerate caustics, grazing or two-bounce limits and uniform lower bounds on the size of phase variation are outside the theorem. Ordinary floating-point agreement does not certify constancy, especially when real variation is below numerical resolution. No validated numerical bounds or proof-assistant formalization are claimed.</p></section>
 <section id="credit"><h2>Context and credit</h2><p>Reznik, Garcia and Koiller recorded the focal product as k₁₁₄. Their final 2021 Arnold Mathematical Journal article, Fifty New Invariants of N-Periodics in the Elliptic Billiard, defines ordinary vertex-to-focus distances and lists the 2-mod-4 observation in Table 2 with an unknown proof entry. Its text does not explicitly resolve primitive least period versus a return list allowing repetitions. The earlier v11 preprint has the different title Eighty New Invariants; editions are not silently substituted.</p><p>Stachel’s published 2022 Theorem 4.3 and equation (4.9) supply the classical parametrization. Standard Jacobi periods, shifts and addition identities, and the general meromorphic Poncelet pole-removal method, retain prior credit. Schwartz, Akopyan–Schwartz–Tabachnikov and Roitman–Garcia–Reznik provide method precedents. The target-specific calculation is the focal divisor and its orbit-coset arithmetic; no new elliptic-function method is claimed.</p><p>Other products have different factors: k₁₁₅ concerns vertices of the outer tangent polygon, k₁₁₇ concerns caustic-contact lengths, and k₂₀₂ concerns perpendicular distances to sides. Ferudun’s related unrefereed note addresses k₁₁₅, rather than the original billiard-vertex product here.</p><p>The source comparison is bounded. No exclusive historical priority, influence, exhaustive literature coverage or present worldwide openness is certified. The full billiard body of the related Dragović–Gajić article remained unavailable, and the inspected author-site Ferudun body was not authenticated as the exact Zenodo-deposit bytes. These limits do not become claims that the unread material is irrelevant.</p></section>
